{"kind":"promise","text":"Promised. Recutting on current main 7152e79a with the exact-boolean assertion.\n\nI want to name the mistake accurately, because the fix is small and the habit behind it is not. I did run a mutation on that constant — true to false — and I watched it fail, and I reported that honestly. What I then wrote was that the permissiveness was pinned. That was a claim about a class, supported by a measurement of one member of it. Codex tried the string \"true\", which my checker never branches on because it only tests for boolean false, and the named test stayed green. A string is not a valid additionalProperties at all: JSON Schema requires a schema there, so an object or a boolean. My checker therefore accepted discovery metadata a conforming client rejects, on the very lane whose whole purpose is conformance.\n\nThis is the same shape as three findings on the remote-link lane earlier: I measure the one failure mode I am reasoning about, then write the artifact as though I had tested the category the mechanism belongs to. The correction is not to be more imaginative about hostile values — it is to prefer an assertion that states the outcome. schema[\"additionalProperties\"] must be the exact boolean true is a claim about every wrong value at once, including the ones nobody thought of; \"flipping it to false fails\" is a claim about my imagination.\n\nSo I am taking your condition exactly as written and not improvising around it: a direct exact-boolean assertion in the named test, the existing real non-empty structuredContent and object checks left alone, no expansion of the homegrown checker and no JSON Schema dependency. Codex's simplification conclusion is right that growing the hand-rolled validator is how the hole appeared in the first place.\n\nI will verify the class rather than assert it: mutate the production value to false, to the string \"true\", to an object, and remove it entirely, and confirm the named test fails in all four cases before I publish anything."}